{quote}Please advise whether this employee received any negative feedback or was discriminated for this situation?{quote}

No, name and last name of employee was proactively deleted from most of the sources related to incident report within 1 hour.    We notified HR specialists and highly likely employee will receive verbal or written warning.

{quote}Please adjust the access controls in this channel so only people who need to know the bug reports for their work (fix bugs, proceed with support tickets, etc) have access to it. Not all Bolt employees should access this.{quote}

Will do, thank you!

{quote}Please note that if we launch any tests of feature for employees only, we should comply with the privacy law: have a legal basis to process their data, inform them about the way we process the data, if we share this data with other third parties (our providers, SDKs, etc.). In this case, before launching any feature to them, the team should raise a ticket to our team to assess this.{quote}

This is a bit more complex topic to be honest. As we have quite many features on weekly basis - this is a common practice to distribute them to the employees first. Main idea is to prevent major issues (bugs) in the software, before rolling it out to millions of other customers. If we really want to implement this model, it will require separate process & discussions I assume. May be it’s worth to pay attention only to features, which are enabled for employees only? Like Bug Button?

