{{https://kibana-logs.prelive.elasticsearch.boltint.net/app/r/s/rNCb6}}

In a way its fixed here: [https://github.com/bolteu/bolt-server/pull/75260|https://github.com/bolteu/bolt-server/pull/75260|smart-link] 



Also I dont think raising error in constructor is right approach anyway.

we can have two methods

{noformat}public check(ctx: Context, input: {}): RuleCheckResult {
  return <true/false>;
}

public check_with_error(ctx: Context, input: {}): RuleCheckResult {
  raise <error if config bad>
  return this.check(ctx, input);
}{noformat}



So when we need to raise error we can create something like this and follow such structure as convention.



In ruby we have methods ending with {{!}} which are used for such dangerous methods, and usage of such method then becomes intentional

